# DFRobot FireBeetle 2 ESP32-P4 (DFR1172), and the AI Kit (DFR1237), which is
# the same board with a camera, a screen and a stand in the box
#
# Build it with:
#   idf.py -B build.firebeetle -D SDKCONFIG=build.firebeetle/sdkconfig \
#     -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;boards/firebeetle2_p4.defaults" build
#
# The smallest board here that can do the whole job: 60 x 25 mm, an
# ESP32-P4NRW32 (32 MB PSRAM), 16 MB flash, an ESP32-C6-MINI-1 for WiFi 6, a
# 15-pin Raspberry Pi camera connector, a microSD slot and two USB-C ports.
# No wired network - the C6 is the only link, like the ESP32-P4-WIFI6.
#
# Read off the vendor schematic (DFR1172_..._schematics_V1.0.pdf, dfrobot.com),
# and cross-checked against Espressif's own board file for it,
# arduino-esp32 variants/dfrobot_firebeetle2_esp32p4/pins_arduino.h. Two sources
# written from the same board, and they agree:
#   ESP32-C6 over SDIO: CLK 18, CMD 19, D0-D3 14/15/16/17, slave reset 54 -
#                       the Function EV numbers, so the same esp-hosted profile.
#   microSD: CLK 43, CMD 44, D0 39, D1 40, D2 41, D3 42, and SD1_VDD through an
#            AO3401 P-channel MOSFET off GPIO 45. The net is even called
#            SD1_PWRN: active low, which is the firmware default.
#   Capture I2C: SDA 7, SCL 8, with 2.2K pull-ups on the CSI connector.
#   BOOT button: GPIO 35. (GPIO 36 is the second strapping pin and is on the
#   header - leave it alone.)
#   Headers: an 18-pin row and a 14-pin row, not a 2x20 - see board_header.c.
#
# The CSI connector is the 15-pin 1.0 mm Raspberry Pi one, in the standard
# order: 1/4/7/10 ground, 2/3 lane 0, 5/6 lane 1, 8/9 clock, 11/12 the camera
# control lines, 13/14 I2C SCL and SDA, 15 3.3 V. A Geekworm C790 ribbon plugs
# straight in. DFRobot's own note is worth repeating: the silver contacts face
# the connector's contacts and the blue backing faces the black clip.
#
# Which USB-C is which matters here, because both are Type-C and they do
# different jobs:
#   - the port beside the RST button carries USB1_P/N, which are the P4's
#     GPIO25/24 - the USB-serial-JTAG. That is power, flashing and the log.
#   - the other one carries USB0_P/N, the P4's DP/DM: USB 2.0 OTG high-speed.
#     That is the target's keyboard and mouse.
# Its VBUS pins tie straight to the board's 5 V rail, so a target that powers
# its port back-feeds the board, exactly as the reference board does down its
# MX1.25 lead: pulling that cable at the target's end reboots the KVM.
#
# NOT YET VERIFIED ON HARDWARE. Configured from the schematic and published so
# owners can try it.

# --- flash and partitions ---------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions_16mb.csv"

# --- silicon revision -------------------------------------------------------
# Left at the base build's pre-3.0 setting: DFRobot's own page says ESP32-P4
# v1.0, so no rev 3.x twin is published for this board.

# --- updates ----------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_KVM_UPDATE_URL="https://espkvm.github.io/espkvm/firmware/firebeetle2-p4/manifest.json"

# --- network ----------------------------------------------------------------
# No Ethernet PHY on this board at all, so the first boot opens the setup
# hotspot and the console can then join a network as a station.
CONFIG_KVM_ETH_ENABLE=n
CONFIG_KVM_WIFI=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_P4_DEV_BOARD_FUNC_BOARD=y
CONFIG_SLAVE_IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_CP_TARGET_ESP32C6=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_AUTO_CALL_INIT_BEFORE_APP_MAIN=n

# --- microSD ----------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_KVM_SD_PWR_GPIO=45

# --- capture reset ----------------------------------------------------------
# The CSI connector's two control lines carry a pull-up and nothing else (10K on
# IO0, IO1 not fitted), so there is no RESETN to drive. The C790 resets itself
# at power-on.
CONFIG_KVM_TC358743_RST_GPIO=-1

# --- console ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Over the USB-serial-JTAG, which is the port used for flashing - so the log is
# readable without a UART adapter, and GPIO 37/38 stay free header pins. This
# board has no UART bridge chip: the programming port is the P4's own.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y

# Which board this is: names it in the console and picks the expansion header
# the Pins tab draws.
CONFIG_KVM_BOARD_FIREBEETLE2_P4=y
